  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "The rate at which guests check into a particular hotel one evening is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=3{t}^{2}+2,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in guests per hour and $t>0$ is the time in hours since $\\text{6}\\,\\text{p.m}.$\n\nGuests are also checking out of the hotel at a steady rate of $1$ guest per hour.\n\nIf there were $200$ guests in the hotel at $6\\,\\text{p.m},$ how many guests will there be in the hotel at $9\\,\\text{p.m?}$",
  "worked_solution": "Guests are entering the hotel at a rate of $3{t}^{2}+2$ guests per hour and are leaving at a rate of $1$ guest per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the number of guests (call it $R(t)$) is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-1 \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}+2-1 \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **total number of guests** after $t$ hours (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =200+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(3{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nTo find out the number of guests after $3$ hours ($9\\,\\text{p.m}$), we substitute $t=3$ into the above, and evaluate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(3) & =200+{\\int }_{0}^{3}(3{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =200+{[{x}^{3}+x]|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =200+[(27+3)-(0)] \\\\  & =200+30 \\\\  & =230\\end{aligned}\n$$",
